{
  // context-email: the Cloudflare Email Worker behind <username>@context.lc.
  //
  // Mail addressed to a username lands in that person's personal context, whose
  // path *is* the username. Shared contexts have no ingestion address — nothing
  // to configure, nothing to disable — so everything from outside passes through
  // one accountable owner before anyone else can see it. See the block comment
  // in src/controlPlane.ts for why that is structural rather than a filter.
  //
  // Cloudflare Email Routing is live on context.lc — MX, SPF and DKIM are
  // verified in DNS — but until this Worker is deployed and bound to a routing
  // rule, nothing receives mail: every message to <name>@context.lc goes
  // nowhere.
  //
  // ── DEPLOYMENT IS NOT COMPLETE WHEN `wrangler deploy` SUCCEEDS ────────────
  //
  // Three things are configured in the Cloudflare dashboard, not here, and the
  // Worker is either wrong or useless without them.
  //
  // 1. ROUTE IT AS A CATCH-ALL, NOT AS PER-ADDRESS RULES.
  //
  //    Email Routing rejects mail for an address with no matching rule, before
  //    the Worker ever runs. If addresses were added one rule at a time, then
  //    Cloudflare's own behaviour would differ between a claimed name and an
  //    unclaimed one — and every refusal in src/index.ts would be uniform for
  //    nothing, because the enumeration oracle would live one layer above it.
  //    The catch-all action must be "Send to a Worker → context-email", so
  //    every address on the domain reaches the same code and gets the same
  //    answer.
  //
  // 2. AUTHENTICATION IS A LABEL HERE, NOT A GATE. READ BEFORE "FIXING" IT.
  //
  //    src/auth.ts believes a message's SPF/DKIM/DMARC verdict only when the
  //    topmost `Authentication-Results` carries this exact authserv-id, is not
  //    folded, is unambiguous, and aligns with `From:`. All of that is intact.
  //    What changed is what happens when it does not hold: the message is
  //    CAPTURED and the note says it was not verified. It is no longer refused.
  //
  //    ── Why, from two real deliveries ────────────────────────────────────
  //
  //    The first was refused `auth_unaligned`. It was an ordinary forward:
  //    `From:` unchanged, the delivering hop's DKIM belonging to the forwarder,
  //    so alignment fails. Cloudflare's activity log read SPF none, DKIM pass,
  //    DMARC none, ARC pass. Forwarding is the product, so that is not a corner
  //    case.
  //
  //    The second, after the sender had configured DKIM for their own domain,
  //    was refused `auth_folded_authentication_results` — because CLOUDFLARE
  //    FOLDS ITS OWN long `Authentication-Results` header, and the anti-forgery
  //    rule refuses every folded one. Worth writing down twice: on this
  //    deployment, the folding rule fires on ordinary mail.
  //
  //    An inbox is expected to contain unverified mail. Refusing it turned a
  //    capture address into something only a sender who had set up DKIM for
  //    their own domain could use, which is almost nobody. So the verdict goes
  //    into the note — `verified`, `sender-authenticated-by`,
  //    `authentication-result`, plus a prose warning that the sender address may
  //    be spoofed — and the message lands.
  //
  //    ── What that settled about the header, which still matters ──────────
  //
  //    This note used to say Cloudflare sends no `Authentication-Results` at
  //    all. It does. `auth_unaligned` was reachable only after the topmost one
  //    had parsed AND matched the authserv-id below. So: the header arrives, it
  //    is topmost, it parses as RFC 8601 with no ARC instance tag, and
  //    `mx.cloudflare.net` is right. (cloudflare/workerd#6740, which reports
  //    none arriving, does not describe this deployment.) The remaining half —
  //    that it appears above any header the SENDER supplied — is still an
  //    assumption about another system, and still the thing to confirm from raw
  //    headers before any `verified: true` label is worth trusting.
  //
  //    ── ARC stays, and still only informs the label ──────────────────────
  //
  //    src/auth.ts reads `ARC-Authentication-Results` (RFC 8617) under five
  //    conditions documented in `verifyViaArc`, of which the load-bearing one
  //    is POSITION: an ARC set is ours only if it sits above the topmost
  //    `Authentication-Results`, inside the block our MX prepended, where a
  //    sender cannot write. DO NOT relax that. An ARC header is plain text with
  //    no signature of its own; without the position rule, trusting one hands
  //    every sender a `verified: true` note for anybody they like.
  //
  //    Whether it ever fires depends on two things nobody has captured: whether
  //    Cloudflare stamps an ARC set of its OWN before Worker delivery, and where
  //    it puts it. To find out, set `LOG_ARC_SHAPE` to "1", send one message,
  //    and read `arc=` on the `captured` line from `wrangler tail` — it rides on
  //    an unverified capture now, there being no authentication refusal left for
  //    it to ride on.
  //
  //      ours=0  — no ARC set of ours reached the Worker. Expected, and the
  //                known limitation: in a real forward the verdict worth having
  //                is sealed by the FORWARDER at i=1, which we have no basis to
  //                trust. Closing that needs an operator-configured list of
  //                sealing domains read from `ARC-Seal: i=1; d=…` under
  //                `cv=pass` — a product decision, not a parser change. See the
  //                comment above `verifyViaArc`.
  //      ours=1  — the ARC path is live, and a forwarded message from a chain we
  //                re-sealed is captured as VERIFIED rather than unverified.
  //
  //    What is NOT true any more: forwarded mail is no longer refused while
  //    that is unsettled. It is captured, unverified, and honestly labelled.
  //
  // 3. VERIFY `OPERATIONS_MAILBOX` AS AN EMAIL ROUTING DESTINATION.
  //
  //    postmaster@ and abuse@ are forwarded there, never ingested. RFC 2142
  //    requires them to stay deliverable and CLAUDE.md records that as
  //    load-bearing. Cloudflare will not forward to an unverified destination,
  //    so an unverified address means those two mailboxes bounce.
  //
  // CUSTOM DOMAINS ARE NOT ATTACHED HERE, and neither is the email route.
  // Same convention as infra/router: attaching them requires the zone to
  // already be on Cloudflare DNS, which a CI deploy cannot do and should not
  // silently try to.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "context-email",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  // A stable past date rather than "today": a future-dated (or clock-skewed)
  // CI runner rejects a compatibility_date that resolves to the future.
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-01",

  "vars": {
    // The Convex HTTP-actions origin: https://<deployment>.convex.site — the
    // `.site` host, NOT the `.cloud` one the client library uses.
    //
    // Public information (it is in every web client's network tab), so it is
    // fine in a public repository.
    "CONTROL_PLANE_URL": "https://clean-ptarmigan-116.convex.site",

    // The apex we accept mail for. A recipient on any other domain is refused
    // before anything else happens.
    "INGEST_DOMAIN": "context.lc",

    // The authserv-id in the `Authentication-Results` header we will believe.
    // Confirmed against a real delivery — see note 2 above.
    "AUTH_SERVICE_ID": "mx.cloudflare.net",

    // "1" to log the bounded ARC shape beside an unverified capture. See note
    // 2: it is how an operator finds out whether the ARC path can fire at all.
    // Integers and one closed enum, never a sender string — but it is a
    // diagnostic, and OFF is its resting state. It was switched on for one
    // question, which the two deliveries in note 2 have now answered well
    // enough to act on; delete it entirely once the `ours=` question is
    // settled from raw headers.
    "LOG_ARC_SHAPE": "",

    // DELIBERATELY EMPTY. postmaster@ and abuse@ are forwarded here; with it
    // unset they are refused and a loud `operations_mailbox_unset` is logged.
    // Set it to a verified Email Routing destination. Not a secret, but it is
    // a real mailbox on a real person's account, so it is left for whoever
    // deploys rather than committed.
    "OPERATIONS_MAILBOX": "",

    // Hard ceiling on a message this Worker will read. Capped again in code at
    // 5 MB regardless of what is set here.
    "MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES": "5000000",

    // Allow-list of native R2 binding names, for self-hosters whose control
    // plane returns a `r2-binding` provider. Empty for the product deployment,
    // whose tenants bring their own buckets over the S3 API.
    "NATIVE_BINDINGS": ""
  },

  // EMAIL_WORKER_SECRET is a secret, set with `wrangler secret put` and never
  // committed. It is NOT `GATEWAY_SECRET`: see the contract block in
  // src/controlPlane.ts for why the two are deliberately separate and what a
  // leak of this one would and would not buy.

  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  }
}
